<fig:schema>

Herein, we propose a sensor-free, self-regulating thermal switch that
leverages the AEE in DyCo$""_5$, a member of the $R$Co$""_5$ family that
is known to exhibit a spin-reorientation transition
(SRT)~@Ohkoshi1977@Tsushima1983@Tereshina2007. In this material, the
magnetic easy axis rotates with temperature due to competing
crystal-field and exchange anisotropies~@Belov1976@Davila2024\; it lies
in-plane for $T < T_(upright(S R 1)) approx 325 thin upright(K)$ and
switches to the $c$ axis at higher temperatures (with
$T > T_(upright(S R 2)) approx 367 thin upright(K)$)~@Ohkoshi1977@Tsushima1983.
As $T$ passes through the SRT interval,
$T_(upright(S R 1)) lt.tilde T lt.tilde T_(upright(S R 2))$, the
magnetization $bold(M)$ reorients from in-plane toward the $c$ axis.
Under a fixed in-plane bias current $bold(J)$, this reorientation
toggles the transverse anomalous Nernst conductivity $alpha_(x y)$ and
thus the AEE-induced heat flux $bold(q) prop alpha_(x y)$, enabling
temperature-thresholded directional switching or modulation of heat flow
without any external sensing. A schematic of the device concept is shown
in Fig.@fig:schema.

To establish the mechanism and quantify performance, we combined
density-functional calculations and the Kubo linear-response formalism
to evaluate the energy-resolved anomalous Hall conductivity
$sigma_(x y) \( epsilon \)$ and the finite-temperature
$alpha_(x y) \( T \)$. We found that although the intrinsic
$sigma_(x y)$ at $E_(upright(F))$ remains sizable for both magnetization
orientations of $bold(M)$, $alpha_(x y)$ changes by several orders of
magnitude across the SRT. This contrast is consistent with the low-$T$
Mott relation through the energy slope
$#scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] partial_epsilon sigma_(x y) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \|_(E_(upright(F)))$~@Mott1936@Mott1969,
reflecting orientation-driven redistribution of Berry curvature near
$E_(upright(F))$. Device-relevant figures of merit, namely the ANE
thermopower $S_(upright(A N E))$ and the Ettingshausen coefficient
$Pi_(upright(A E E)) = T thin S_(upright(A N E))$~@Seki2018, exhibit
robust, orientation-controlled switching at a fixed temperature (e.g.,
$300 thin upright(K)$), highlighting a materials-integrated route to
compact, current-driven, sensor-free thermal control.

#strong[Theoretical methods.] We performed density functional theory
(DFT) calculations using the full-potential linearized-augmented
plane-wave (FP-LAPW) method~@blaha2001wien2k. Spin--orbit coupling (SOC)
is treated self-consistently within the second-variational FP-LAPW
scheme. Unless otherwise noted, the rare-earth $4 f$ states are treated
in the generalized gradient approximation (GGA)~@Perdew1996 with a
Hubbard correction $U$~@Liechtenstein1995, and a moderate on-site
interaction on Co $3 d$ is used only to test the robustness of trends.
We utilized the Wannier90 program~@Pizzi2020 to construct maximally
localized Wannier functions (MLWFs), which were then employed for
Wannier interpolation to evaluate the Berry curvature.
MLWFs~@Marzari2012 are constructed primarily from Co $3 d$ and $s$
states (with rare-earth $f$) to reproduce the DFT bands. Longitudinal
thermoelectric properties were computed within semiclassical Boltzmann
transport theory as implemented in BoltzTraP2~@Madsen2018. Two
magnetization configurations were considered throughout:
$bold(M) #h(-1em) parallel #h(-1em) c$ and
$bold(M) #h(-1em) perp #h(-1em) c$ (in-plane). Brillouin-zone (BZ)
quantities were evaluated on a dense $68 times 68 times 73$ $k$-mesh;
all energy-resolved and finite-$T$ integrals were examined for $k$-mesh
convergence.

The intrinsic anomalous Hall conductivity (AHC) and anomalous Nernst
conductivity (ANC) are computed from the $k$-space Berry curvature
within the Kubo linear-response formalism:
$ sigma_(x y) #h(0em) = #h(0em) - e^2 / ℏ sum_n #h(-1em) integral_(upright(B Z)) #h(-1em) frac(d^3 bold(k), \( 2 pi \)^3) #h(0em) f_(n bold(k)) thin Omega_(n \, z) \( bold(k) \) \, $<eq:ahc_def>
$ alpha_(x y) #h(0em) = #h(0em) e / ℏ sum_n #h(-1em) integral_(upright(B Z)) #h(-1em) frac(d^3 bold(k), \( 2 pi \)^3) #h(0em) Omega_(n \, z) \( bold(k) \) thin s_(n bold(k)) \, $<eq:anc_def>
where
$f_(n bold(k)) = f #h(-1em) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] epsilon_(n bold(k)) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)]$
is the Fermi--Dirac factor and
$s_(n bold(k)) = - #h(-1em) [f_(n bold(k)) ln f_(n bold(k)) + \( 1 - f_(n bold(k)) \) ln \( 1 - f_(n bold(k)) \)]$
is the entropy density per state~@Xiao2006@Xiao2010. The Berry curvature
follows from
$ bold(Omega)_n \( bold(k) \) #h(0em) = #h(0em) nabla_(bold(k)) times bold(A)_n \( bold(k) \) \, #h(2em) bold(A)_n \( bold(k) \) #h(0em) = #h(0em) i thin #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[chevron.l] u_(n bold(k)) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\|] nabla_(bold(k)) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\|] u_(n bold(k)) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[chevron.r] \, $<eq:berry_defs>
where $\| u_(n bold(k)) chevron.r$ are cell-periodic Bloch states.
Equations~#link(<eq:ahc_def>)[\[eq:ahc\_def\]]--#link(<eq:berry_defs>)[\[eq:berry\_defs\]]
fix our sign and derivative conventions.

For interpretability we also employ the energy resolved AHC
representation,
$ alpha_(x y) #h(0em) = #h(0em) - frac(1, e T) integral d epsilon #h(0em) #scale(x: 180%, y: 180%)[\(] - frac(partial f, partial epsilon) #scale(x: 180%, y: 180%)[\)] thin \( epsilon - mu \) thin sigma_(x y) \( epsilon \) \, $<eq:alpha_energy>
which reduces in the low temperature limit
($k_B T lt.double E_(upright(F))$) using the Sommerfeld expansion to the
Mott relation:
$ alpha_(x y) #h(0em) tilde.eq #h(0em) - frac(pi^2 k_B^2 T, 3 e) thin frac(partial sigma_(x y) \( epsilon \), partial epsilon)\|_(epsilon = E_(upright(F))) . $<eq:mott>
Equation~#link(<eq:mott>)[\[eq:mott\]] is used as an #emph[intuition
aid]\; all quantitative results are obtained from
Eqs.~#link(<eq:anc_def>)[\[eq:anc\_def\]] and
#link(<eq:alpha_energy>)[\[eq:alpha\_energy\]] at finite $T$. We next
connect $alpha_(x y)$ to device relevant thermoelectric metrics.

Device relevant metrics, the transverse thermopower due to ANE and the
Ettingshausen coefficient, are related by the Kelvin relation
$Pi_(upright(A E E)) = T thin S_(upright(A N E))$. Under open circuit
conditions in the transverse direction and for a small Hall angle, we
use~@Ikhlas2017
$ S_(upright(A N E)) #h(0em) approx #h(0em) alpha_(x y) / sigma_(y y) #h(0em) - #h(0em) S_(y y) thin sigma_(x y) / sigma_(y y) \, $<eq:sane_smallangle>
where $sigma_(y y)$ and $S_(y y)$ are the longitudinal conductivity and
Seebeck coefficient along the current direction. While AHC and ANC are
determined by the Berry curvature in our formulation, the longitudinal
conductivity depends on scattering. We therefore compute
$sigma_(y y) \/ tau$ and $S_(y y)$ using BoltzTraP2 within the constant
relaxation time approximation. In this approximation $S_(y y)$ is
insensitive to the absolute value of $tau$, whereas $sigma_(y y)$
requires $tau$. We estimate $tau$ by comparing calculated
$sigma_(y y) \/ tau$ with reported experimental conductivities of
$R$Co$""_5$ compounds, and adopt $tau = 11.3$~fs and $tau = 12.7$~fs as
representative values. Representative values are summarized in
Table~@tab:tau. We report $S_(upright(A N E))$ and $Pi_(upright(A E E))$
for both values to indicate the uncertainty associated with $tau$.

