#strong[Berry curvature origin of the contrast.] To identify the
microscopic origin of the orientation contrast, Fig.~@fig:bands_BC
presents the band dispersion and the Berry curvature along the high
symmetry path $Gamma arrow.r M arrow.r K arrow.r Gamma arrow.r A$ for
$bold(M) parallel c$ and $bold(M) perp c$. The Berry curvature shown in
the lower panels is the sum over the occupied states,
$Omega_z \( bold(k) \) = sum_n f \( epsilon_(n bold(k)) \) thin Omega_(n \, z) \( bold(k) \)$,
which corresponds to the integrand of the intrinsic anomalous Hall
conductivity up to a constant prefactor. In both magnetization
configurations, $Omega_z \( bold(k) \)$ is nearly zero over most of the
path but exhibits two strongly peaked hot spots on the $Gamma arrow.r A$
segment with opposite signs. These peaks originate from spin orbit
coupling gaps at band (avoided) crossings among Co $3 d$ derived states,
for example involving $d_(z^2)$ with $d_(x z)$ and
$d_(x^2 - y^2) \/ d_(x y)$ characters, which generate large
$Omega_(n \, z)$. Upon reorienting $bold(M)$, the hot spot intensity is
redistributed and their positions shift slightly along
$Gamma arrow.r A$, leading to a modest change in the separation between
the two peaks. This redistribution reshapes $sigma_(x y) \( epsilon \)$
near $E_F$ and hence modifies the energy slope that controls
$alpha_(x y)$, providing a consistent explanation of the trends in
Fig.~@fig:DyCo5_calc\(a,b). A detailed microscopic analysis of the
origin of the positional shift is beyond the scope of the present work
and is left for future study.

